Shah Jahan Begum</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Jahan Begum was ruler of Bhopal State in central India, first as a child under her mother Sikandar Begum's regency from 1844 and in her own right from 1868 until…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-mansur"><span class="enc-name">Shah Mansur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Mansur was a prince of the Muzaffarid dynasty that ruled Fars and central Iran in the 14th century, and its last effective ruler, seizing Shiraz in 1387.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-mir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-mir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shah Mir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Mir was the founder of the Shah Mir dynasty and of the Kashmir Sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-mosque"><span class="enc-name">Shah Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shah Mosque, also known as the Imam Mosque, is a great congregational mosque on the Naqsh-e Jahan Square in Isfahan, built under Shah Abbas I in the early…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-muhammad-sagir"><span class="enc-name">Shah Muhammad Sagir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Muhammad Sagir was a Bengali poet, probably of the reign of Ghiyasuddin Azam Shah around 1400, and one of the earliest known Muslim poets of the Bengali language.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-murad"><span class="enc-name">Shah Murad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Murad was the first emir of Bukhara of the Manghit dynasty, ruling from 1785 until his death in 1800.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-nawaz-bhutto"><span class="enc-name">Shah Nawaz Bhutto</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Nawaz Bhutto was a politician from Sindh who became diwan, or chief minister, of Junagadh State in 1947.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-nimatullah-wali"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-nimatullah-wali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shah Nimatullah Wali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Nimatullah Wali was a Persian Sufi master and poet, born in Aleppo, who settled at Mahan near Kerman in Iran, where his shrine stands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-rukh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shah Rukh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Rukh was a son of Timur who ruled the Timurid Empire from 1409 to 1447, governing from Herat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukn-e-alam"><span class="enc-name">Shah Rukn-e-Alam</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Rukn-e-Alam was a Sufi saint of the Suhrawardi order in Multan, grandson of Bahauddin Zakariya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-shuja-muzaffarid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-shuja-muzaffarid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shah Shuja (Muzaffarid)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Shuja was ruler of the Muzaffarid dynasty in southern Iran from 1358 to 1384, after deposing and blinding his father Mubariz al-Din Muhammad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-shuja-durrani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-shuja-durrani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shah Shuja Durrani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Shuja Durrani was a ruler of the Durrani Empire, reigning from 1803 to 1809 and again from 1839 to 1842.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-suwar"><span class="enc-name">Shah Suwar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shah Suwar was a ruler of the Dulkadirids, a Turkmen beylik on the frontier between Mamluk Syria and Ottoman Anatolia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahaji Bhonsle</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahaji Bhonsle was a Maratha general in the Deccan and father of Shivaji, founder of the Maratha kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahanshah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahanshah</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahanshah, meaning King of Kings, was the title of the supreme ruler in ancient and medieval Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahnameh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahnameh</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shahnameh, or Book of Kings, is the national epic of Iran, completed by the poet Ferdowsi in about 1010 after some thirty years of work.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahr-province"><span class="enc-name">Shahr (province)</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">A shahr was a province of the Sasanian Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahr-yagil-yuhargib"><span class="enc-name">Shahr Yagil Yuhargib</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahr Yagil Yuhargib was a king of Qataban, an ancient South Arabian kingdom with its capital at Timna in present-day Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahrbaraz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahrbaraz was a leading Sasanian general under Khosrow II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahrisabz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahrisabz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahrisabz</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahrisabz is a city in southern Uzbekistan, south of Samarkand, in the Kashkadarya valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahrizor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahrizor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahrizor</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahrizor is a fertile plain in the Kurdistan Region of north-eastern Iraq, east of Sulaymaniyah in the foothills of the Zagros.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahrokh-shah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahrokh-shah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahrokh Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahrokh Shah was a grandson of Nader Shah who reigned as a nominal Afsharid ruler in Mashhad after being blinded.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahrud-river"><span class="enc-name">Shahrud River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shahrud is a river of northern Iran that flows through the Alborz mountains and joins the Qezel Owzan at Manjil to form the Sefidrud, which runs to the Caspian Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahrukh-bi"><span class="enc-name">Shahrukh Bi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahrukh Bi was the leader of the Ming tribe of Uzbeks in the Ferghana valley and founder of the Khanate of Kokand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahu-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahu-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahu I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahu I was the fifth Chhatrapati of the Maratha Empire, a grandson of Shivaji.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahu-of-kolhapur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shahu-of-kolhapur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shahu of Kolhapur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahu of Kolhapur, born Yeshwantrao Ghatge in the family of the chiefs of Kagal, was adopted into the Kolhapur royal house in 1884 and ruled the princely state from…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahverdi-khan-khorshidi"><span class="enc-name">Shahverdi Khan (Khorshidi atabeg)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shahverdi Khan was the last atabeg of the Khorshidi dynasty, which ruled Lesser Luristan in western Iran from the fortress of Falak-ol-Aflak at Khorramabad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaivism"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shaivism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shaivism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Shaivism is the Hindu tradition that worships Shiva as the supreme deity.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shajar al-Durr</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shajar al-Durr was the widow of the Ayyubid sultan al-Salih Ayyub, who ruled Egypt as sultan in 1250.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaka-zulu-king"><span class="enc-name">Shaka (Zulu king)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shaka was the king of the Zulu who built the Zulu kingdom into a powerful military state in south-eastern Africa from the 1810s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shakanda"><span class="enc-name">Shakanda</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shakanda was a king of Makuria in Nubia, installed by the Mamluks in 1276.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shakhbut-bin-sultan-al-nahyan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shakhbut-bin-sultan-al-nahyan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shakhbut bin Sultan Al Nahyan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shakhbut bin Sultan Al Nahyan was ruler of Abu Dhabi from 1928 to 1966.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shaki, Azerbaijan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sheki is a city in northern Azerbaijan on the southern slopes of the Greater Caucasus, long a stop on regional trade routes and a centre of silk production.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaktism"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shaktism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shaktism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Shaktism is a major tradition of Hinduism that worships the goddess, Devi or Shakti, as the supreme power of the universe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shakushain"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shakushain.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shakushain</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shakushain was an Ainu chief of the Shibuchari region in south-eastern Hokkaido who led the largest Ainu uprising against Japanese control.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shakya-zangpo"><span class="enc-name">Shakya Zangpo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shakya Zangpo was the first ponchen, or civil administrator, of Tibet under the Sakya regime, appointed with the backing of the Mongol Yuan dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shalfak"><span class="enc-name">Shalfak</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shalfak was a Middle Kingdom Egyptian fortress in Lower Nubia, built on a granite outcrop above the Nile in the Second Cataract region.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shallum-of-israel"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shallum-of-israel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shallum of Israel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shallum was king of Israel for one month around 752 BCE, according to the Bible.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shalmaneser I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shalmaneser I was a king of Assyria in the thirteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shalmaneser III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shalmaneser III was king of Assyria from 859 to 824 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-v"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-v.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shalmaneser V</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shalmaneser V was king of Assyria from 727 to 722 BCE, son and successor of Tiglath-Pileser III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shama-ghana"><span class="enc-name">Shama, Ghana</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shama is a coastal town in the Western Region of Ghana, at the mouth of the Pra river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shamakhi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shamakhi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shamakhi is a town in the mountains of eastern Azerbaijan, for centuries the capital of the Shirvanshahs and of the province of Shirvan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamanism"><span class="enc-name">Shamanism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Shamanism refers to religious practices centred on a shaman, a figure believed to communicate with spirits through trance and ritual.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamash-resha-usur"><span class="enc-name">Shamash-resha-usur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shamash-resha-usur was a governor of Suhu and Mari on the middle Euphrates in the first half of the 8th century BCE, nominally subject to Babylon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamash-shum-ukin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shamash-shum-ukin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shamash-shum-ukin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shamash-shum-ukin was king of Babylon from 668 to 648 BCE, a son of the Assyrian king Esarhaddon, who made him ruler of Babylonia under his younger brother Ashurbanipal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shambaa-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shambaa-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shambaa people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shambaa are a Bantu-speaking people of the Usambara Mountains in the Tanga Region of north-eastern Tanzania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shammar-yahrish"><span class="enc-name">Shammar Yahrish</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shammar Yahrish was a king of Himyar in south Arabia in the late 3rd and early 4th centuries CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shams al-Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shams al-Dawla was the Buyid ruler of Hamadan and the surrounding Jibal from 997 to about 1021, a son of Fakhr al-Dawla.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-din-ali-ibn-masud"><span class="enc-name">Shams al-Din Ali ibn Mas'ud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shams al-Din Ali ibn Mas'ud was the founder of the Mihrabanid dynasty of maliks in Sistan, on the borders of present-day Iran and Afghanistan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-din-eldiguz"><span class="enc-name">Shams al-Din Eldiguz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shams al-Din Eldiguz was a former Kipchak slave who founded the Eldiguzid dynasty of atabegs of Azerbaijan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-din-muhammad-kart"><span class="enc-name">Shams al-Din Muhammad Kart</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shams al-Din Muhammad Kart was the first malik of the Kart dynasty of Herat, of a family linked to the Ghurid Sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamsher-prakash"><span class="enc-name">Shamsher Prakash</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shamsher Prakash was Raja of Sirmur, a princely state in the Himalayan foothills of present-day Himachal Pradesh, from 1856 to 1898.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shamshi-Adad I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shamshi-Adad I was an Amorite ruler who reigned c. 1808–1776 BCE and built a kingdom across northern Mesopotamia, including the city of Assur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-ahmad-khan"><span class="enc-name">Shamsuddin Ahmad Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shamsuddin Ahmad Khan was nawab of Loharu and Firozpur Jhirka in northern India, son of the state's founder Ahmad Bakhsh Khan.</span></a></li></ul>
